\section{XY Interface}{


The \strong{XY} interface coordinates parasite
\strong{Transmission Dynamics}
through mosquito biting and blood feeding, and computes the dynamical terms
that pass information between the
\strong{X} and \strong{Y} processes. The XY interface has three parts:
\itemize{
\item \link{BloodFeeding} (see the \href{https://dd-harp.github.io/ramp.xds/articles/BloodFeeding.html}{Blood Feeding vignette}) -- mosquitoes \emph{search} for blood hosts, so the algorithms that model
blood feeding were developed around the concept of blood host availability. Models can set
up constant blood feeding rates, but the parameter values should satisfy some constraints:
\itemize{
\item the blood feeding rate should be 0 if there are no hosts available;
\item the human fraction should be 0 if there are no humans available;
\item the human fraction should be 1 if only humans are available;
}
\item \link{Transmission} (see the \href{https://dd-harp.github.io/ramp.xds/articles/Transmission.html}{Transmission vignette}) -- under the blood-feeding model, the biting
distribution matrix (\eqn{\beta}) ensures that every bite and blood meal
is accounted for:
\itemize{
\item it updates the local fraction (if there are visitors)
\item it computes the local
daily entomological inoculation rate (EIR or \eqn{E}) for all the strata
\item it computes net infectiousness (NI or \eqn{\kappa}) in each patch.
}
\item \link{Exposure} (see the \href{https://dd-harp.github.io/ramp.xds/articles/Exposure.html}{Exposure vignette}) -- handles mosquito blood feeding from the human or host perspective. The function
computes the force of infection (FoI) for all the population strata from:
\itemize{
\item the local daily EIR (\eqn{E})
\item the travel EIR (\eqn{\delta})
\item an assumption about the distribution of the expectation
\item a model of partial immunity (\eqn{b}) from the \strong{X} component
}
}
}

\section{ML Interface}{


\strong{Mosquito Ecology:} the \strong{L} and \strong{M} processes communicate through the habitat interface. Aquatic
habitats are located in the patches and configured during basic setup by passing the habitat membership vector. See the \href{https://dd-harp.github.io/ramp.xds/articles/EggLaying.html}{Habitats and Mosquito Ecology vignette}.
\itemize{
\item \strong{Patch Dynamics} -- adult mosquito populations are distributed in patches (see \link{xds_info_patch_dynamics})
\item \strong{Aquatic Habitats} -- aquatic habitats are located in patches (see \link{xds_info_aquatic_habitats})
\itemize{
\item \link{Emergence} --- a function that uses the habitat matrix to aggregate adults emerging from aquatic habitats to patches
\item \link{EggLaying} --- a function that uses the habitat matrix and habitat search weights to distribute eggs laid by adults in a patch among the aquatic habitats in that patch.
}
}
